Here is that - "Computer Training Centre Management System — Complete Requirement & Functional Plan
We need to build a professional internal management system for an organisation that operates computer-training activities through two distinct sections under one Super Admin: Youth Computer Centre and School Computer Program. This is primarily a management/admin system, not a public-facing institutional website. The system should be designed as a real production management application with a clean, simple, responsive interface and a strong focus on correct data relationships, security, performance, low unnecessary database reads, and future maintainability. The implementation technology can remain within the existing preferred Firebase-based environment unless there is a strong technical reason to use something else. The building AI should investigate the requirements, identify the best architecture, and implement the system properly rather than merely following a superficial page-by-page CRUD approach.
Organisation and user hierarchy
At the top of the entire system there will be one Super Admin, representing the head of the organisation/centre. The Super Admin has complete visibility and control over the organisation.
Under the Super Admin there are two separate operational sections:

Youth Computer Centre
This section can contain multiple computer-training branches/youth centres. For example, Branch A, Branch B, Branch C, etc.
School Computer Program
This section can contain multiple schools where the organisation provides computer education to school students. For example, School 1, School 2, School 3, etc.
The structure should therefore remain simple and explicit:
SUPER ADMIN
│
├── YOUTH COMPUTER CENTRE
│ ├── Branch A
│ ├── Branch B
│ └── Branch C
│
└── SCHOOL COMPUTER PROGRAM
├── School 1
├── School 2
└── School 3
There is no need to build an unnecessarily generic multi-tenant framework or make every possible organisation type dynamically configurable. The system only needs to properly support these two known sections.
Super Admin
The Super Admin can access the entire organisation and can see data from every Youth Computer branch and every School Computer unit.
The Super Admin should be able to create and manage Youth Computer branches and School Computer schools, manage their respective heads/users, create the courses and batches used by the Youth Computer section, view students and admissions across all branches, view and manage fees and payments, access reports, and manage archived data.
The Super Admin dashboard should not display the entire organisation's Youth and School data simultaneously by default, because that would make the dashboard unnecessarily crowded.
Instead, the top navigation should contain a prominent system switcher beside the notification/profile area.
For example:
[ Youth Computer Centre ▼ ] 🔔 Super Admin

When Youth Computer Centre is selected, the dashboard and sidebar show only Youth Computer-related information.
When School Computer is selected, the dashboard and sidebar change to show only School Computer-related information.
The default section can be Youth Computer Centre, since that is the primary structured training operation.
This switch should change the context of the entire dashboard, statistics, lists, reports and relevant navigation rather than merely filtering one table.
Super Admin sidebar
The sidebar should remain simple and should not be overloaded with unnecessary options.
For Youth Computer Centre, the primary options are:

Dashboard
Branches
Courses
Batches
Students
Admissions
Fees
Payments
Reports
For School Computer, the relevant options are:
Dashboard
Schools
Students
Fees
Payments
Reports
There should also be an Archives section for old/completed Youth Computer batches.
The Super Admin management area should contain only:
Branch Heads
Organisation Details
There is no need for separate sidebar items such as Create User, Manage Accounts, Email Settings, System Settings, etc. If those functions are necessary, they should be incorporated naturally inside Branch Heads, Organisation Details, or the relevant management screens.
Attendance is not part of this system and must not be added anywhere unless explicitly requested later.
Youth Computer Centre structure
The Youth Computer Centre is the more structured training operation.
The Super Admin creates the branches. Each branch represents an actual Youth Computer Centre/branch.
A branch should have information such as branch name, address, contact information, branch head information, branch status and other necessary organisational details.
Each branch is associated with a Branch Head.
The Branch Head account is linked to one particular branch. When the Branch Head logs in using their authorised email/account, the system must automatically know which branch they belong to and show only that branch's data.
For example:
Branch Head: Bishnupur Centre
branchId: BISHNUPUR_001
That Branch Head must only be able to access Bishnupur-related students, admissions, fees, payments, etc.
A Branch Head must not be able to access another branch's students simply by changing a URL, query parameter, document ID or manipulating the frontend.
The access restriction must be enforced at the backend/database security level as well as in the user interface.
Course management
Only the Super Admin can create, edit, activate/deactivate and manage Youth Computer courses.
Branch Heads cannot create their own courses.
The organisation currently has multiple computer courses and different durations. The actual course catalogue should be configurable by the Super Admin based on the institute's requirements rather than hardcoded into the interface.
Examples from the current course material include courses such as CITA, CDTP, CFAS, DITA, DDTP, etc., with different durations and fee structures.
The system should support information such as:

Course name
Course code, if required
Duration
Course description
Eligibility
Course fee
Installment structure
Course status
Other relevant academic/administrative information
The Super Admin can update the course catalogue, while Branch Heads simply use the available courses during admission.
Batch management
Only the Super Admin creates and manages batches.
A batch belongs to a course and can have its own relevant details such as batch name/identifier, course, duration, timing, start date, end date where applicable, branch availability and status.
The exact design should allow the organisation to maintain multiple batches without forcing Branch Heads to create their own batch structures.
Branch Heads can select from the batches made available by the Super Admin when admitting students.
This prevents every branch from creating inconsistent versions of the same course or batch.
Branch Head permissions
A Branch Head is responsible for operating their own branch.
They can:
Take new admissions
Register students
View their branch's students
Search their branch's students
Assign students to available courses
Assign students to available batches
Manage student information
View course/fee information relevant to their branch
Record student payments
View payment history belonging to their branch
Generate/view receipts where implemented
Access branch-specific reports
They cannot:
Create or modify organisation-wide courses
Create or modify organisation-wide batches
Create branches
Access other branches
See another branch's students
See another branch's payment history
Create another Branch Head
Modify Super Admin settings
Change organisation-wide configuration
The security model must enforce these permissions server-side/database-side, not just hide menu items.
Student management
Students should be maintained as proper individual records rather than creating separate duplicate records whenever their course changes.
A student should have a unique student identifier/registration ID.
The student record may contain:
Student ID
Name
Photo, if required
Date of birth where required
Gender where required
Contact information
Address
Guardian/parent information where applicable
Admission date
Branch
Current course
Current batch
Course history
Fee information
Payment history
Status
Other required admission information
The system should maintain the student's history properly.
Course upgrade / continuation
This is a very important business requirement.
A student may initially enrol in a shorter course and later decide to continue into a longer-duration programme.
For example, a student may initially take a 6-month course.
After completing the 6 months, the student may upgrade/continue into a 12-month programme.
The system must understand that the student has already completed the first 6 months.
It should not treat the student as a completely new student starting from zero.
Similarly:
6-month → 12-month
Completed = 6 months
Remaining = 6 months
or:
6-month → 18-month
Completed = 6 months
Remaining = 12 months

The student's course history should retain the previous programme and the new continuation/upgrade.
The system should also support appropriate fee adjustment based on the institute's actual fee policy. For example, if the student has already paid for the original 6-month programme, the system should calculate/record only the additional amount applicable to the upgraded programme rather than blindly charging the entire new course fee again.
The exact financial rules should be configurable according to the institute's policy.
The student's previous payments, receipts and course history must remain connected to the same student.
Admission management
Branch Heads should be able to take admissions.
During admission, the Branch Head should select from the courses and batches already created by the Super Admin.
The admission process should create the student record and associate the student with the correct branch, course and batch.
The system should maintain the admission information separately enough that admission reporting can be performed, while avoiding unnecessary duplicate student data.
The Super Admin should be able to view admissions across all Youth Computer branches.
A Branch Head should see admissions only from their own branch.
Fees and Payments must be separate
The system should not combine Fees and Payments into one overloaded section.
There should be two separate sidebar options:
Fees
This section is primarily concerned with a student's current fee status and collecting a new payment.
Payments
This section is the complete transaction history/ledger.
This distinction is important both for usability and performance.
Pay Fees
The Pay Fees screen should not load all students and their fee information just because the admin wants to collect one student's payment.
The admin should first enter the student's unique Student ID.
Only after the Student ID is provided should the system fetch the relevant student's required information.
For example:
Enter Student ID
↓
Find Student
↓
Show current fee information

The screen can then show:

Student name
Student ID
Course
Batch
Total course fee
Amount already paid
Current installment amount
Current outstanding amount
Previous payment information
Next payment due date
Payment status
Relevant payment history
Record Payment option
The admin can then record the payment.
After successful payment, the system should correctly update the student's financial status and create a transaction record.
The implementation should be designed so that a payment operation cannot leave the student record and transaction record in an inconsistent state.
Payment History
The Payments section is the complete transaction history.
For the Super Admin, this should contain payments from all Youth Computer branches when the Youth Computer system is selected.
The Super Admin should be able to filter/search by useful criteria such as:
Branch
Date/date range
Student ID
Student name
Course
Batch
Payment method
Transaction/reference number
Other genuinely useful filters
The Super Admin should have an overall organisation-wide transaction view, with branch filtering.
For a Branch Head, the Payment History should show only transactions from that Branch Head's own branch.
The Branch Head should not have access to another branch's payment history.
Payment history should be paginated and queried efficiently. The system should not download the entire historical payment collection into the browser unnecessarily.
Payment records
Each transaction should maintain sufficient information to reconstruct the payment history, including appropriate information such as:
Transaction/payment ID
Student ID
Student name/reference
Branch
Course/batch reference
Amount
Date/time
Payment method
Installment information
Previous due/current due where relevant
Transaction/reference number
Recorded by
Receipt reference where applicable
The exact database structure should be designed by the implementation AI after analysing these requirements.
School Computer Program
The School Computer Program is not the same business model as the Youth Computer Centre.
This is a separate section under the same Super Admin.
Structure:
SUPER ADMIN
│
└── SCHOOL COMPUTER PROGRAM
├── School 1
├── School 2
└── School 3
The organisation provides computer education to school students.
These students do not necessarily enrol into a fixed 6-month/12-month/18-month course.
Instead, this is essentially a continuous monthly-payment model.
A student can continue participating without a predefined course completion date.
Therefore, the Youth Computer course/batch/upgrade model should not be unnecessarily forced onto School Computer students.
The School Computer system should primarily manage:

Schools
Students
School/class information where required
Student status
Monthly fee
Monthly payment history
Payment status
Other necessary school-program information
There is no requirement for the School Computer section to use the Youth Computer course-duration logic.
School Computer fees
The School Computer program uses a monthly payment model.
A student's financial record should therefore support:
Monthly fee
Previous payments
+
Current month status
+
Outstanding amount
+
Next payment/month

The system should allow the organisation to continue collecting monthly fees without requiring the student to be assigned a fixed course duration.
Again, the exact payment workflow should be designed efficiently rather than duplicating the Youth Computer fee architecture unnecessarily.
School Computer payment history
The School Computer section should have its own Payment History.
The Super Admin can view payments across all schools.
If school-level users/heads are introduced, they should only see their own school's data.
The same principle of pagination and targeted queries should apply.
Dashboard behaviour
The dashboard should be context-sensitive.
When:
Youth Computer Centre is selected:
The dashboard should show useful Youth Computer information such as:

Number of active branches
Number of courses
Number of active batches
Total students
Total admissions
Fee collection
Outstanding fees
Relevant recent admissions
Recent payments
Outstanding student fees
Branch-wise student/collection summaries
Other genuinely useful Youth Computer metrics
When:
School Computer is selected:
The dashboard should change to show School Computer-specific information such as:
Number of schools
Active students
Monthly fee collection
Outstanding monthly fees
Recent admissions/registrations if applicable
Recent payments
School-wise summaries
Other useful school-program metrics
The dashboard should not make unnecessary database reads simply to display decorative statistics.
Only useful metrics should be queried.
Archive system
The organisation may accumulate old batch data over several years. Keeping every historical student record, document and image permanently in the live Firebase database/storage may eventually increase storage usage and make the operational database unnecessarily large.
Therefore, the system should include an Archive mechanism.
The primary use case is for completed/old Youth Computer batches.
The archive workflow should be something like:
Old Batch
↓
Select Archive
↓
Generate complete archive
↓
Download archive
↓
Verify archive
↓
Delete old live data
The archive should not merely export a plain JSON file if that would make historical data inconvenient to use.
The preferred approach is a self-contained offline archive.
For example:
Batch-2026-27-Archive.zip

├── index.html
├── css/
├── js/
├── data/
│ ├── students.json
│ ├── payments.json
│ ├── admissions.json
│ ├── batch.json
│ └── other-data.json
├── images/
│ ├── student-001.jpg
│ └── student-002.jpg
└── documents/
└── ...

After extracting the ZIP, the administrator should be able to open:
index.html

and view the archived data locally.
The archive viewer should provide a useful read-only interface for the historical batch, including student records, photos and relevant historical information.
It should not require Firebase or an internet connection merely to view the already-downloaded archive, provided browser security restrictions are handled appropriately by the implementation.
The archive must include the relevant images/documents rather than merely retaining Firebase Storage URLs that will stop working after deletion.
The archive process must be designed carefully so that the system does not delete the live batch until the archive has been successfully generated and verified/downloaded.
The final implementation should consider a safe confirmation workflow and preferably prevent accidental deletion.
Email functionality
The system should support automated email communication from an organisation-controlled email address.
The organisation can provide/configure an appropriate Gmail/Google Workspace account, or another suitable SMTP provider.
The system may send relevant emails such as:

Admission confirmation
Payment confirmation
Fee reminders
Receipt-related emails
Administrative notifications
Branch/account invitation emails
Other genuinely useful transactional emails
The implementation should not assume unlimited email sending.
If Gmail is used, the implementation should respect Google's sending limits and avoid spam-like behaviour.
The email sender should be configurable in a secure manner and credentials/secrets must never be exposed in frontend code.
Authentication
There should be secure authentication for the Super Admin and Branch Heads.
A Branch Head should receive/use the authorised email associated with their branch.
When they log in, their account must be mapped to the correct branch.
The application must never determine permissions solely from data supplied by the browser.
The backend/database security layer must enforce:
Super Admin
→ All Youth Computer branches
→ All School Computer units
Youth Branch Head
→ Own Youth branch only

School-level user, if implemented
→ Own school only

The exact authentication method and account invitation process can be chosen by the implementation AI based on the safest and most maintainable Firebase approach.
Data isolation and security
This is one of the most important technical requirements.
A Branch Head must not be able to access another branch's information even if they manually manipulate requests, URLs, IDs or frontend JavaScript.
The same applies to payments and student records.
For example:
Branch A Head
↓
Branch A Students ✓
Branch A Payments ✓
Branch B Students ✗
Branch B Payments ✗

The Super Admin has organisation-wide access.
Security rules and backend logic should therefore be designed around the actual role and branch relationship.
The implementation AI should perform a security review after building the system.
Firebase/database efficiency
The system should be designed with Firestore read efficiency in mind.
Do not make large collection reads just to populate dashboards or payment screens.
Examples:
Bad approach:
Pay Fees
→ Download every student
→ Search in browser
→ Find Student ID

Preferred approach:
Pay Fees
→ Enter Student ID
→ Query the required student
→ Fetch only relevant financial information

Similarly, Payment History should use:

Pagination
Targeted queries
Appropriate indexes
Date/branch filters
Limited result sets
Dashboard statistics should be designed intelligently rather than repeatedly reading hundreds or thousands of documents.
The implementation AI should choose the best Firestore data model and indexing strategy after analysing the complete workflow.
General UI/UX requirements
The interface should be professional, clean and practical rather than overly decorative.
It should work properly on:
Desktop
Laptop
Tablet
Mobile
The Super Admin should immediately understand which system they are currently managing through the Youth Computer Centre / School Computer switcher.
The interface should avoid unnecessary menus, unnecessary animations, excessive cards and unnecessary data displayed everywhere.
The purpose is an actual daily-use management system.
Important scope boundaries
The following are not currently required:
Public institutional website
Public course website
Attendance
Student-facing public portal
Unnecessary dynamic organisation-type builder
Unnecessary role types
SMS integration unless separately requested
WhatsApp API integration unless separately requested
Complex accounting software
Unnecessary HR/payroll system
Unnecessary inventory system
Unnecessary examination management system
The implementation AI should not add large unrelated modules simply because they are technically possible.
At the same time, it should not interpret this document as limiting its engineering ability. If a better internal architecture, data model, security mechanism, caching strategy, transaction approach, indexing strategy or UI solution is appropriate, it should use its expertise while preserving the business requirements above.
Core principle
The system should be built around these fundamental relationships:
SUPER ADMIN
│
┌────────────────┴────────────────┐
│ │
▼ ▼
YOUTH COMPUTER CENTRE SCHOOL COMPUTER PROGRAM
│ │
Multiple Branches Multiple Schools
│ │
Branch Heads Students
│ │
Students Monthly Fees
│ │
Courses + Batches Payments
│
Admissions
│
Fees
│
Payments
│
Course Continuation/
Upgradation
The most important objective is to build a reliable, secure and efficient management system for the organisation's actual workflow, rather than simply creating a collection of CRUD pages.
The developer/AI should first inspect the complete requirements, establish the data model and permission model, identify relationships and edge cases, and then implement the system module-by-module with regression-safe testing. Existing working functionality should not be broken when new modules are introduced."

